      class="case-category cs-wide"
      role="tabpanel"
      aria-labelledby="tab-capstone-case"
      hidden
    >
      <figure class="cs-hero-media cs-wide capstone-hero-thumb pb-8">
        <Image
          format="png"
          src={facultyEvalRecommendations}
          alt="Tutor evaluation dashboard — key findings, overview, and suggestions & recommendations"
          width={3000}
          quality={100}
          class="rounded-xl w-full h-auto object-contain"
        />
      </figure>

      <div class="cs-meta cs-wide">
        <MetaItem label="Project type">
          <p class="cs-inline-result">Capstone project, AI evaluation</p>
        </MetaItem>
        <MetaItem label="My role">
          <p class="cs-inline-result">UX Designer</p>
        </MetaItem>
        <MetaItem label="Tools">
          <Chip title="Figma" level="professional" showLevel={false}>
            <Icon slot="icon" name="figma-brands-solid-full" size={18} aria-hidden />
          </Chip>
          <Chip title="Claude" level="professional" showLevel={false}>
            <Icon slot="icon" name="claude-brands-solid-full" size={18} aria-hidden />
          </Chip>
        </MetaItem>
      </div>

      <StatRow>
        <Stat value="4 mo" label="project timeline" />
        <Stat value="4" label="users in moderated prototype testing" />
      </StatRow>

      <CaseStudyQuestion question="How can faculty trust an AI tutor before students ever see it?">
        <p class="project-description">
          UT Sage lowers the barrier for faculty to configure AI tutors, but there
          was no reliable way to test quality, misconceptions, and pedagogy before
          students used a tutor. New faculty also had no onboarding to learn the
          evaluation tool.
        </p>
      </CaseStudyQuestion>

      <SectionHeading index="01" title="Goal" id="capstone-goal" />
      <div class="cs-section-body">
        <p class="project-description">
          Faculty could publish a tutor, but had no repeatable way to test
          how it would actually teach.
        </p>
        <div class="cs-card-grid">
          <Card title="How might we?" cardType="outline">
            <p>
              Help faculty confidently assess and refine a tutor before it
              goes live, with clear evidence, explainable ratings, and
              updates they can approve?
            </p>
          </Card>
          <Card title="Design goal" cardType="outline">
            <p>
              Pair approachable onboarding that builds a mental model of each
              configuration field with an evaluation dashboard that takes
              faculty from high-level warnings to granular checks,
              suggestions, examples, and simulated dialogue.
            </p>
          </Card>
        </div>
      </div>

      <SectionHeading
        index="02"
        title="Research & evaluation framework"
        id="capstone-research"
      />
      <div class="cs-section-body">
        <p class="project-description">
          I paired secondary research with historical interview data from UT Sage
          papers to uncover configuration gaps, then translated AI evaluation
          benchmarks into a scorecard instructors can read, anchored in classroom
          teaching moves.
        </p>
        <div class="cs-card-grid">
          <Card title="8 evaluation dimensions: response quality" cardType="outline">
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1.5">
              <li><strong>D1 Instruction-following:</strong> honors faculty special instructions.</li>
              <li><strong>D2 Truthfulness:</strong> keeps explanations accurate and avoids hallucinated content.</li>
              <li><strong>D3 Style / tone:</strong> language is warm yet academically rigorous.</li>
              <li><strong>D4 Visual reasoning:</strong> interprets and reasons about diagrams or graphs.</li>
              <li><strong>D5 Visual perception:</strong> reads visual inputs shared by learners.</li>
              <li><strong>D6 Calibration:</strong> pacing fits the learner profile.</li>
              <li><strong>D7 Conciseness:</strong> keeps replies scoped appropriately.</li>
              <li><strong>D8 Emotional responsiveness:</strong> addresses frustration constructively.</li>
            </ul>
          </Card>
          <Card title="7 tutoring skills: instructional behaviors" cardType="outline">
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1.5">
              <li><strong>T1</strong> Identifies misconceptions explicitly.</li>
              <li><strong>T2</strong> Guides with questions instead of giving answers outright.</li>
              <li><strong>T3</strong> Uses examples and analogies to demystify ideas.</li>
              <li><strong>T4</strong> Offers alternate solution paths.</li>
              <li><strong>T5</strong> Scaffolds step-by-step when problems are dense.</li>
              <li><strong>T6</strong> Recalls syllabus or uploaded resources when relevant.</li>
              <li><strong>T7</strong> Accurately judges correct and incorrect steps in learner work.</li>
            </ul>
          </Card>
        </div>
      </div>

      <SectionHeading
        index="03"
        title="Prototyping"
        id="capstone-prototyping"
      />
      <div class="cs-section-body">
        <p class="project-description">
          I iterated in Figma on two connected surfaces: tutorial overlays that
          explain how Create, Configure, and Train map to tutor memory, and an
          evaluation concept that surfaces key findings before the detail tabs.
        </p>
        <div
          class="capstone-proto-grid grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4"
        >
          <figure class="capstone-figure">
            <figcaption
              class="text-sm pb-2 text-[rgba(var(--foreground-color),0.72)] font-medium"
            >
              Welcome modal: sets expectations and scope guardrails.
            </figcaption>
            <Image
              format="png"
              src={facultyEvalTutorialModal}
              alt="Welcome to Create Your UT Sage Tutor modal"
              width={2400}
              quality={100}
              class="rounded-xl w-full h-auto object-contain bg-[rgba(var(--floating-color),0.35)]"
            />
          </figure>
          <figure class="capstone-figure">
            <figcaption
              class="text-sm pb-2 text-[rgba(var(--foreground-color),0.72)] font-medium"
            >
              Orientation step: contextualizes Create, Configure, and Train.
            </figcaption>
            <Image
              format="png"
              src={facultyEvalTutorialStep}
              alt="Tutorial explaining Create Configure and Train sections"
              width={2400}
              quality={100}
              class="rounded-xl w-full h-auto object-contain bg-[rgba(var(--floating-color),0.35)]"
            />
          </figure>
          <figure class="capstone-figure">
            <figcaption
              class="text-sm pb-2 text-[rgba(var(--foreground-color),0.72)] font-medium"
            >
              Chat scaffolding: an alternative to typing every field manually.
            </figcaption>
            <Image
              format="png"
              src={facultyEvalChatStep}
              alt="Tutorial tooltip on tutor creation chat syncing fields"
              width={2400}
              quality={100}
              class="rounded-xl w-full h-auto object-contain bg-[rgba(var(--floating-color),0.35)]"
            />
          </figure>
          <figure class="capstone-figure">
            <figcaption
              class="text-sm pb-2 text-[rgba(var(--foreground-color),0.72)] font-medium"
            >
              Configure cue: anchors the fields that feed calibration logic.
            </figcaption>
            <Image
              format="png"
              src={facultyEvalConfigureStep}
